    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. if it is a command signature, it shouldn't just be a continuous stream; it would probably be bursty, right? like a discrete update sent at specific intervals to minimize the noise.\n\ni'm thinking we should look at the \"inter-arrival time\" of these orbital adjustments. if we plot the time gaps between the non-stochastic corrections, we can look for a mathematical distribution that doesn't match any known natural process (like something following a Poisson distribution, which you'd expect from random noise). \n\nis there a way to formulate a statistical test to detect a latent periodicity or a specific bit-rate in these timing intervals? i want to see if we can mathematically define a threshold where the \"burstiness\" of the corrections becomes statistically significant enough to imply an encoded 'ping' or command sequence from an external source.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
